   "evidence": "Declaration: `theorem mul_prod_mem_pow_of_mul_mem ... (P : Ideal S \u2192 A \u2192 Prop) (htop : \u2200 x, P \u22a4 x) (hmul : \u2200 {I x y}, P I x \u2192 P J y \u2192 P (I * J) (x * y)) ... : P (J ^ Fintype.card \u03b9) (g * \u220f i, f i)`. Neither the hypotheses nor the conclusion contains `\u2208`. In the naming conventions, `mem` stands for `\u2208`, and a lemma whose conclusion is an arbitrary `Prop`-valued motive should have `induction` in its name.",
   "file": "TauCeti/RingTheory/Ideal/Operations.lean",
   "fix": "Rename it as an induction principle on the predicate, e.g. `Ideal.pow_card_mul_prod_induction`. Update the call sites in `Derivation.iterate_mul_prod_mem_ideal_pow`, `Polynomial.hasseDeriv_prod_mem_ideal_pow` and the docstring entry for this lemma.",
   "issue": "`Ideal.mul_prod_mem_pow_of_mul_mem` claims both a membership conclusion (`mem_pow`) and a membership hypothesis (`of_mul_mem`). Neither is in the statement: the conclusion is `P (J ^ card \u03b9) (g * \u220f i, f i)` for an arbitrary `P : Ideal S \u2192 A \u2192 Prop`, and `hmul` is the closure condition `P I x \u2192 P J y \u2192 P (I * J) (x * y)`. In effect this is an induction principle over a predicate, not a membership lemma.",
   "line": 216
  }
